On Sunday 19 January 2003 05:49 pm, John Goerzen wrote:
> Hi,
>
> I have an AlphaPC 164LX system with 256MB RAM that I'm wanting
> to expand to 512MB.  I have tried to find out, on various
> Internet sites, just what kind of RAM I need, and have found
> only conflicting information.  Indeed, popping in a standard
> PC133 DIMM from another PC was totally unrecognized by the
> Alpha.
>
> Certain sites seem to indicate that the 164LX uses proprietary
> RAM, and off-the-shelf stuff will not work.  Others say that
> off-the-shelf stuff WILL work, if chosen correctly.  Can
> anyone help me out?

For off-the-shelf parts, the PC164LX needs *unbuffered* ECC=20
PC100, installed in matching pairs.  The first pair should be=20
installed in the two slots closest to the CPU.   PC133 _should_=20
be backwards compatible, but I've seen certain brands (like=20
Viking Components) that weren't as backwards compatible as they=20
should have been, even on x86 boxen with trusty 440BX chipsets.

AFAIK the 164LX only takes up to 256MB DIMMs.  512MB DIMMs might=20
work if you managed to find them in unbuffered ECC (not bloody=20
likely), but you'd be taking a stride into the unknown there. =20
Tread with care.

My 164LX came with 512MB already (more than enough for me atm),=20
but i have a friend who has done the upgrade.  He dropped in=20
four 256MB sticks from http://www.crucial.com that fit the=20
specs, and they all work fine.  I'll see if I can get the exact=20
model number from him, so you can buy with that warm fuzzy=20
feeling of knowing it will work. ;)

--=20
Kelledin
